<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The host <-> plugin contract.

    This assembly exists for one reason: it is the only type identity the monitor
    and a plugin can agree on. An interface compiled into both sides is two
    interfaces, and the disagreement does not fail the build. It fails later, as an
    InvalidCastException thrown from a cast nobody could check, on a plugin the user
    installed.

    Direction of travel: no behaviour. Nothing in here decides anything, opens
    anything, fills in a default or falls back to something plausible. It declares
    what a plugin supplies and what the host is allowed to ask of it. A capability
    no measured backend has is not declared, and one that does is not left out:
    IExecutionAdapter.Capabilities is the flag set the host greys out against, and
    it is also the data behind the published capability table.

    This project references nothing, deliberately. No ProjectReference, no
    PackageReference. A reference out of here is how a contract stops being a
    contract, so the test suite asserts that there is none.

    Precedent: ICpuStateSource, declared apart from IMemoryBackend on CpuSnapshot.cs
    because a backend with no processor must not pretend it has one. The same
    discipline, generalised: a plugin supplies what it can and the host asks what it
    has.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>WinASM65.Monitor.Abstractions</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>WinASM65.Monitor.Abstractions</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>